LanguageTool for Collabora Online

LanguageTool is a grammar, style and spelling checker for English, French,
German, Spanish and some thirty other languages. Collabora Online talks to
it over HTTP, the way it talks to the Duden Korrekturserver and Grammalecte,
and underlines what it finds as you type. This directory shows how to run
it next to Collabora Online.

The service is the collabora/languagetool image on Docker Hub, built from
https://github.com/CollaboraOnline/docker-languagetool from the release
tag of LanguageTool, since LanguageTool publishes no binary archive after
version 6.6. The image is free to use, but unsupported, and it is the open
source LanguageTool: the rules the premium version adds are not in it (see
https://languagetool.org/premium?a=collabora). LanguageTool is released
under the LGPL 2.1; the licence text is in the image.

Try it:

    docker run --rm -p 8010:8010 collabora/languagetool
    curl -d 'text=The%20catt%20sat%20down.&language=en-US' http://127.0.0.1:8010/v2/check

The answer lists a spelling mistake in "catt".

Running it next to Collabora Online:

    cd docker/languagetool
    docker compose up

compose.yaml starts the collabora/code image with the checker enabled and
pointed at the service, on a network the service shares with nothing else.
In coolwsd.xml the same two settings are

    <languagetool>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <base_url>http://languagetool:8010/v2</base_url>
    </languagetool>

The base URL ends in /v2, the version of the LanguageTool API; the checker
appends /check and /languages. A language is checked when the checker's
language list has it and the server does. allowed_languages in coolwsd.xml
does not limit this: it only decides which local dictionaries, hyphenation
patterns, thesauri and Lightproof rules are loaded at start. For the
languages the server spell checks, it takes over from the local spell
checker where the local one has the language.

The same settings reach the premium service at languagetool.org, with the
account's name in user_name and its key in api_key.

The service has no authentication and no TLS, and it answers any origin.
Anyone who can reach it can send it text, so keep it on an internal
network, as compose.yaml does, and never on a public address.

The server runs on Java, as the user nobody, and wants half a gigabyte of
memory or so. The JAVAOPTIONS environment variable goes to the Java
runtime, as in JAVAOPTIONS=-Xmx768m, and EXTRAOPTIONS to the server; its
options are listed at https://dev.languagetool.org/http-server. A server
configuration property is set with an environment variable of its name
prefixed with LT_, as in LT_maxTextLength=50000. LanguageTool finds more
confused words with n-gram data, which is large (several gigabytes per
language) and is not in the image: unpack it on the host, following
https://dev.languagetool.org/finding-errors-using-n-gram-data, and mount
the directory at /ngrams, as the commented lines in compose.yaml show.

The server settings show in the Options dialog only where the
administrator allows it, with allow_user_settings in coolwsd.xml.

Collabora Office, the desktop application, has the same checker and is
configured in its own Options dialog.
